DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The family of DCE 1.1 compliant UUID functions allow applications to op‐
     erate on universally unique identifiers, or UUIDs.  The uuuuiidd__ccrreeaattee() and
     uuuuiidd__ccrreeaattee__nniill() functions create UUIDs.  To convert from the binary
     representation to the string representation or vice versa, use
     uuuuiidd__ttoo__ssttrriinngg() or uuuuiidd__ffrroomm__ssttrriinngg() respectively.

     The uuuuiidd__ttoo__ssttrriinngg() function set _*_s_t_r to be a pointer to a buffer suffi‐
     ciently large to hold the string.  This pointer should be passed to
     free(3) to release the allocated storage when it is no longer needed.

     The uuuuiidd__eenncc__llee() and uuuuiidd__eenncc__bbee() functions encode a binary representa‐
     tion of a UUID into an octet stream in little-endian and big-endian byte-
     order, respectively.  The destination buffer must be pre-allocated by the
     caller, and must be large enough to hold the 16-octet binary UUID.  These
     routines are not part of the DCE RPC API.  They are provided for conve‐
     nience.

     The uuuuiidd__ddeecc__llee() and uuuuiidd__ddeecc__bbee() functions decode a UUID from an octet
     stream in little-endian and big-endian byte-order, respectively.  These
     routines are not part of the DCE RPC API.  They are provided for conve‐
     nience.

     The uuuuiidd__ccoommppaarree() and uuuuiidd__eeqquuaall() functions compare two UUIDs for
     equality.  UUIDs are equal if pointers _a and _b are equal or both NULL, or
     if the structures _a and _b point to are equal.  uuuuiidd__ccoommppaarree() returns 0
     if the UUIDs are equal, -1 if _a is less than _b, and 1 if _a is greater
     than _b.  uuuuiidd__eeqquuaall() returns 1 if the UUIDs are equal, 0 if they are not
     equal.

     The uuuuiidd__iiss__nniill() function compares a UUID to NULL.  The function returns
     1 if _u is NULL or if the UUID consists of all zeros, and zero otherwise.

     The uuuuiidd__hhaasshh() function returns a 16-bit hash value for the specified
     UUID.

RREETTUURRNN VVAALLUUEESS
     The successful or unsuccessful completion of the function is returned in
     the _s_t_a_t_u_s argument.  Possible values are:

     uuid_s_ok                   The function completed successfully.

     uuid_s_bad_version          The UUID does not have a known version.

     uuid_s_invalid_string_uuid  The string representation of an UUID is not
                                 valid.

     uuid_s_no_memory            The function can not allocate memory to store
                                 an UUID representation.

     uuuuiidd__ccoommppaarree(), uuuuiidd__eeqquuaall(), uuuuiidd__iiss__nniill(), and uuuuiidd__hhaasshh() always set
     _s_t_a_t_u_s to uuid_s_ok.

